<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nallur Kandaswamy Kovil</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nallur Kandaswamy Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nallur">Nallur</a> Kandaswamy Kovil is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Murugan</a> at Nallur, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. The original temple, linked with the kings of Jaffna, was destroyed by the Portuguese in the 17th century; the present building was raised in the 18th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule. Its annual festival is one of the largest gatherings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamils</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nallur-kandaswamy-kovil.jpg" alt="Nallur Kandaswamy Kovil" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gane Kumaraswamy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nallur_Kandasamy_front_entrance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first Kandaswamy temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nallur">Nallur</a> was founded in 948 CE. According to the Yalpana Vaipava Malai, the temple was developed on the site in the 13th century by Puvenaya Vaku, a minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga-magha">Kalinga Magha</a>. A third temple is credited to Sapumal Kumaraya, an adopted son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakramabahu-vi">Parakramabahu VI</a> who ruled the Jaffna kingdom on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayawardenepura-kotte">Kotte</a> kingdom. Nallur was then the capital of the Jaffna kings, and the royal palace stood close to the temple, which also served as a fort with high walls.</p><p>Filipe de Oliveira, the Portuguese conqueror of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna-kingdom">Jaffna Kingdom</a>, destroyed the third temple in 1624. Its site is now occupied by St. James' Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nallur">Nallur</a>, in whose vicarage part of the original Shivalingam was kept until 1995, when it was destroyed during the recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> by the Sri Lankan armed forces. Cankilian Thoppu, the facade of the palace of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cankili-ii">Cankili II</a>, still stands in Nallur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The fourth and present temple was built in 1734, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule, by Don Juan Ragunatha Maapaana Mudaliyar, a shroff in the Dutch Katchery. It began as a building of brick and stone with a cadjan roof and two small halls, enshrining a vel, the divine spear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Murugan</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> named Krishnaiyar was its first priest. The founder's descendants have kept the temple as its custodians ever since. The 7th custodian, Arumuga Maapaana Mudaliyar, built the first bell tower in 1899, paved the sanctum in granite in 1902 and raised the first enclosing wall in 1909.</p><p>Kumaradas Maapaana Mudaliyar, the 10th custodian from 1964 until his death in 2021, virtually rebuilt the whole complex, which became the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Its main entrance faces east under a five-storey gopuram in the Dravidian style. The nine-storey Shanmuha Raja Gopuram was unveiled on 21 August 2011, and the Gubera Raja Gopuram, which opened a northern entrance, on 4 September 2015.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The annual festival lasts twenty-five days and begins with the hoisting of the holy flag, the Kodiyetram; for centuries the cloth has been brought to the temple in a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> by a family of the Sengunthar Kaikola Mudaliyar. Its best-attended event is the Ther Thiruvila, the chariot festival, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Murugan</a> and his consorts are carried out on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> throne made in 1900. The temple is a central institution of Tamil identity in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and the diaspora has built temples of the same name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nallur Kandaswamy temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court supported Hindu temples, including <b>Nallur Kandaswamy</b>, and Tamil literature on astrology and medicine.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaffna-kingdom">Jaffna Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
